From: dtrace.nix maintainers
Subject: bpf: bound memory while preparing wildcard probe programs

Program construction is intentionally done before global maps are created so
that code generation can discover strings, variables, and other map sizing
requirements.  Keeping every constructed DIFO alive until the later load pass,
however, makes memory proportional to the number of matched probes.  A wildcard
FBT enabling on a generic x86_64 kernel can retain more than 150,000 programs
and exhaust over 16 GiB before loading begins.

Treat the existing phases as analysis and emission passes: construct and free
each DIFO during analysis, then reconstruct, link, load, attach, and free one
probe at a time during emission.  Compiler-wide metadata discovered in the
first pass remains in the DTrace handle, while peak DIFO memory is bounded by a
single probe program.  Preserve add_probe calls for providers without their own
trampolines: USDT providers use that hook to populate runtime dispatch maps.

diff --git a/libdtrace/dt_bpf.c b/libdtrace/dt_bpf.c
--- a/libdtrace/dt_bpf.c
+++ b/libdtrace/dt_bpf.c
@@ -1286,7 +1286,7 @@ dt_bpf_make_progs(dtrace_hdl_t *dtp, uint_t cflags)
 
 		DT_DISASM_PROG(dtp, cflags, dp, stderr, NULL, prp->desc);
 
-		prp->difo = dp;
+		dt_difo_free(dtp, dp);
 	}
 
 	return 0;
@@ -1340,10 +1340,17 @@ dt_bpf_load_progs(dtrace_hdl_t *dtp, uint_t cflags)
 		if (prp->prov->impl->add_probe)
 			prp->prov->impl->add_probe(dtp, prp);
 
-		dp = prp->difo;
-		if (dp == NULL)
+		if (prp == dtp->dt_error ||
+		    prp->prov->impl->prog_type == BPF_PROG_TYPE_UNSPEC)
 			continue;
 
+		dp = dt_construct(dtp, prp, cflags, NULL);
+		if (dp == NULL)
+			goto fail;
+		prp->difo = dp;
+
+		DT_DISASM_PROG(dtp, cflags, dp, stderr, NULL, prp->desc);
+
 		if (dt_link(dtp, prp, dp, NULL) == -1)
 			goto fail;
 
 		DT_DISASM_PROG_LINKED(dtp, cflags, dp, stderr, NULL, prp->desc);
